# Building a Scalable Data Pipeline for Cloud Migration Using Spark and Snowflake

## Introduction  
As a data engineer, I recently led a project focused on migrating large-scale data from an on-premise Hadoop system to Snowflake. This migration aimed to improve query performance, simplify data access, and enable real-time analytics. Here's how I designed and optimized the pipeline.

## Tech Stack  
- Apache Spark  
- Informatica  
- Snowflake  
- Avro/Parquet formats  
- AWS S3  
- SQL & Python  

## Challenges Faced  
- Handling schema evolution during data transformation  
- Optimizing data transfer to reduce cloud storage and compute costs  
- Ensuring data quality, consistency, and security  

## Solution Approach  

### 1. Data Extraction & Format Conversion  
I used Spark jobs to extract structured and semi-structured data (Avro/ORC) and converted it into optimized Parquet format.

### 2. Transformation & Cleaning  
Using Spark and Informatica, I applied business rules, anonymized sensitive data, and filtered corrupted records.

### 3. Cloud Upload & Snowflake Integration  
The pipeline uploaded data into S3 buckets and used Snowflake's `COPY INTO` command for ingestion. I implemented auto-scaling and partitioning for better performance.

### 4. Automation  
Scheduled jobs using Apache Airflow for orchestration and included alerts for job failure or data anomalies.

## Impact  
- Reduced data processing time by 40%  
- Enabled business teams to access insights in near real-time  
- Improved overall pipeline reliability and maintainability  

## Conclusion  
This project reinforced the importance of scalable design, performance tuning, and end-to-end automation in modern data pipelines. I’m now exploring ways to integrate LLMs for metadata generation and automated documentation.
